5) D. Zhu, X. Wei, G. Zhao, W. Wu, H. Zou, J. Ran, X. Wang, L. Sun, X. Zhang, and S. Li Chain-of-thought matters: improving long-context language models with reasoning path supervision. CoRR abs/2502.20790. External Links: cite156†Link†doi.org , cite157†Document†dx.doi.org , 2502.20790 Cited by: cite158†§4 , cite108†§6.1 .\nL293: ## Appendix A Experiment Details\nL294: #### Implementation Details\nL295: We implement our reinforcement learning pipeline based on the VeRL framework (cite77†Sheng et al., 2024 ). For rollout generation, we set the sampling temperature to $1.0$, with a maximum context window of 120k tokens for input and 8k tokens for output generation. The group size is configured as $G=6$. Optimization is performed with a global batch size of 64 and a mini-batch size of 32, utilizing a constant learning rate of $2\\times 10^{-6}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.10306] Evidence-Augmented Policy Optimization with Reward Co-Evolution for Long-Context Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10306)\nciteturn30929view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10306\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\n"
